contentText:
As the record industry continues to reshape itself through a bevy of layoffs and reorganizations at some of its biggest companies, so too has the U.S. recorded music market share landscape shifted repeatedly in the past year.

After the third quarter of 2026, that has meant officially shifting Universal Music Group’s divisions into two label groups: REPUBLIC Collective, which includes Island, Mercury and Big Loud, and now Def Jam and MCA; and Interscope Capitol, which includes Interscope Geffen A&M (IGA) and Capitol Music Group (CMG), as well as Capitol Christian, Astralwerks and Blue Note. We’ve also seen the further consolidation of Atlantic Music Group (AMG), which moving forward will include indie distributor ADA and its 0.52% market share under 10K Projects’ number, and thus Atlantic’s. (In this report, ADA remains separate but will fall under AMG in the future after the division was moved under AMG CEO Elliot Grainge ’s purview last month.)&nbsp;

