Having said that, a surging Virgin Music Group, boosted by the incorporation of Downtown Music Holdings and rebounded numbers from its twin frontline label groups, led to Universal Music Group’s growth through the first three quarters of the year. UMG now has a 35.23% current market share, down a bit from the 35.96% it held three quarters of the way through 2025 but up considerably from its Q1 (30.76%) and Q2 (32.44%) marks . Within UMG, REPUBLIC commands its usual industry-leading mark, reaching a 14.05% current share through the first nine months of the year, roughly comparable with the same time last year (when Def Jam and MCA were separate) and representing quarter-on-quarter increases, from a 9.98% share in Q1 and 11.43% at midyear, now that those labels are officially in the fold. Under REPUBLIC’s purview, Island (2.15%), Big Loud (2.00%) and Mercury (1.39%) continue to shine, with the latter riding high on Noah Kahan ’s huge The Great Divide , the No. 3 album of the year so far, and Morgan Wallen , who is co-released with Big Loud.&nbsp;

Interscope Capitol, meanwhile, consolidated its hold on the second spot, posting a 13.46% current share. Within that, IGA accounted for 8.71% and CMG for 4.74%, with the former down year over year and the latter ticking up over the first nine months of 2025. Interscope Capitol is also down year over year from what it would have combined for in 2025, though it has also grown across each quarter of this year as parent company UMG returns to growth itself. Due to all the changes at the UMG over the past 12 months, year-over-year and quarter-over-quarter comparisons for individual labels are more difficult than usual.&nbsp;

In third place is the Atlantic Music Group — which encompasses Atlantic Records, 300 Entertainment and 10K Projects — with an 8.52% current share through the first three quarters of the year. That’s a jump from the 7.83% share that AMG commanded at the same point last year, with the third quarter of 2025 being the first that incorporated 10K into its market share. The boost came largely from the Atlantic Records share, which has seen success this year with Don Toliver , whose OCTANE album is among the year’s biggest, along with releases from Bruno Mars , Stella Lefty &nbsp;and more that have peppered the upper echelons of the charts for much of the year.&nbsp;

Rising to fourth is Columbia Records, which has had a monster year thanks to breakout artist Ella Langley and her “Choosin’ Texas” smash, which has spent 25 weeks atop the Hot 100 to date, a record for the chart. That brought Columbia to a 5.28% current share through the first nine months of 2026, up from 4.54% this time last year, allowing the label to leapfrog Warner Records. Warner — which includes Warner Nashville, Warner Latin and Rhino in its share — comes in fifth, at a 5.10% current share, down from the 6.13% it commanded nine months into 2025. With both UMG and WMG combining each of their labels into groups, the rest of the top 10 is all Sony, with RCA (3.00%, down from 4.22%), Alamo Records (2.04%, down from 2.54%), Sony Nashville (1.81%, down from 1.83%), Sony Latin (1.54%, down from 2.11%) and Epic Records (1.49%, down from 1.64%) round out the current share top 10 rankings.&nbsp;

After Universal’s lead among the label groups, Sony Music came in second in current share at 25.69%, down from 26.65% a year ago; and Warner Music Group came in third at 16.67%, down from 16.87% this time last year. The lower numbers for all three majors meant a boom for the indie sector, which grew from 20.55% through the first three quarters of 2025 to a 22.41% current share in 2026 by distribution ownership. (Though major label executives privately point to the rise of “AI slop” for some of that decline.) By label ownership, the current market share number for the indies stands at 43.64%, based on Billboard calculations from Luminate data. By that metric, Universal would stand at 26.73%, Sony at 15.50% and Warner at 14.13%.&nbsp;

In terms of overall market share — meaning all releases regardless of age — Universal Music Group commanded 39.22% of the market by distribution ownership. That’s up from last year’s 38.46% thanks to continued strength from its catalog share, which reached 40.33% (up from 39.23%) so far this year. Sony stood tall in second at 27.65%, essentially flat year over year, while Warner dropped slightly to 18.70% from an 18.97% mark through the first nine months of 2025. The indies also dipped in overall share, down to 14.44% from 14.94% over the same period in 2025.&nbsp;

Overall share also shakes up the top 10 among the individual labels, with Interscope Capitol (15.41%) holding a significant margin over REPUBLIC (13.41%) among the top two slots, while Atlantic came in third with 8.97%. Warner Records stands in fourth in overall share, at 6.69%, ahead of Columbia’s 5.21% and RCA’s 4.61%. Interscope Capitol, Warner and Columbia all dropped year over year, with REPUBLIC and Atlantic inching up, though once again the realignments make year over year comparisons tougher to square directly.&nbsp;

As is often the case, given that catalog consumption remains much stronger than current, the catalog rankings broadly align with the overall marks. Behind Universal’s industry-leading 40.33% mark (a jump from 39.23% last year), Sony commanded 28.19% in catalog, up from 27.94% in 2025, while Warner slipped to 19.26% from 19.64% and the indies fell to 12.22% by distribution ownership from 13.19%.&nbsp;
